WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:16.070
- Words of the world are
the life of the world.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.000
That line was first
spoken by Wallace Stevens

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.320
in his poem, "An Ordinary
Evening in New Haven,"

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.243
not far from here, 70 years
ago, November 4th, 1949.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.300
Beinecke Library has
the typescript sheets,

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.050
the original typescript
that Mr. Stevens read from

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.130
on that ordinary evening in New Haven,

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.650
and they're accompanied
by a manuscript note,

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.440
a handwritten note.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.950
The papers are at Beinecke and Yale

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:48.390
because of Mrs. Irwin
Goodenough, Evelyn Goodenough,

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.090
whose husband was the
president of the academy,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.310
headquartered three blocks
down Prospect Street.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.870
And the note says,

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.250
Stevens gave the sheets to Mrs. Goodenough

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.747
who had sat beside the poet and quote,

00:01:01.747 --> 00:01:04.517
"studied his stage-fright

00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:07.530
"during the earlier parts of the program."

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.120
Archives give us extraordinary access

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:13.380
to the humanity of the
people represented there,

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.290
and I have to say,

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.630
this morning, I'm glad to know

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.300
that a future Pulitzer Prize winner

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:24.300
had anxiety even amidst the
friendly audience 70 years ago.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:28.980
I first read that poem myself
when I was a rising junior

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.840
in Yale college during a summer course

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.040
on modern American poetry.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.420
And to riff off of Goethe
and the late great Yalie,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.690
Jerry Pelican, what we have
inherited from our parents,

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:46.030
we just possess again in
order to make it our own.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:49.450
That in many ways is the
work of the university.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.200
And that poem, I have possessed

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.440
again and again and again and again

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.350
and have made it my own.

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.960
If you happen to follow me on Instagram,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.237
you know, ordinary evening
or afternoon or morning,

00:02:03.237 --> 00:02:06.963
"In New Haven" is my go-to
verse, even this morning.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.840
Now, today is my 13,076th day
at Yale. (audience laughs)

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.750
I've a few ordinary evenings,
mornings, afternoons,

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.820
and even late nights here.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.190
I've walked the halls of
this place for 36 years

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:29.500
of its more than 318 years of existence.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.660
I've been a New Havener
for 11% of the days,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.760
since John Davenport first
preached under a huge oak tree,

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.750
not far from here.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.510
And I acknowledge, moreover,
that we gather on land,

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:46.960
that the Quinnipiac people
occupied, cultivated,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.390
and stewarded for thousands of years.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.320
In fact every day at home,

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:54.220
I look out over the Quinnipiac meadows

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.980
toward Grannis Island,
which was the seasonal home

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.000
for the Quinnipiac people.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.450
And as I look out in the
evening or in the morning,

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.720
I'm reminded, as the great
scholar John Menta said,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.860
that the Quinnipiac people
were people who lived lives

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.970
where thanksgiving was a daily ritual,

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:19.080
a cosmology that has much
to offer us even today.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.230
So I know a little bit about this place

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:23.650
and about its history and maybe that's why

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.260
Franz and Julie invited me
to come here this morning

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.120
and to share with you.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.880
But first, some housekeeping
before we proceed.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.040
With me, I invite you
to close your laptops,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.213
stow away your mobile phones,
stash your PlayStations,

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.700
remove your VR goggles, and disregard

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.113
all digital devices for
the duration of this talk.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:56.550
As a little incentive, you
all should have jotters

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.963
that you can make notes on.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.190
And if you're watching this on tape,

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:02.800
no, you don't get a jotter.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.863
There is a benefit for showing up.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.880
So, I ask that we get out of the cloud

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.570
and rest comfortably
here on the common ground

00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:18.480
of Kroon Hall, on this
campus, in New Haven.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.623
And that we listen together for clarity,

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.380
amidst the indistinct
chatter that is so much

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.340
our digitally mediated lives,
that we seek as it were,

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.510
signal amidst the noise.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.320
I'm joining you in this adventure

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.050
of Yale digital unplugged.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:41.140
I will inflict no
PowerPoint slides on you.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.070
I'm not gonna read from a tiny screen

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:44.950
between me and you.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:47.140
And I might even make eye contact

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.400
as a sign of respect that you are due

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.010
in this shared reality
that we make together.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:55.790
For you are part of this program,

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:58.413
not a mere consumer of data.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.760
As my friend Kae Sun, great
Ghanaian-Canadian musicians

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.757
tweeted a few months ago,

00:05:04.757 --> 00:05:09.697
"The real conspiracy is to turn everyone

00:05:09.697 --> 00:05:11.277
"into a spectator."

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:14.840
Today, let's all be participants,

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:16.923
with our minds open and active.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:21.320
Now, Franz and Julie actually
invited me as Franz noted,

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.970
because we shared a
great adventure together

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.700
over the last year and a half or so,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.880
to renew and refresh the
Beinecke Library website,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.730
which I think is the biggest
single, all-in project,

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:36.050
that Yale sites has done to date.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.730
And I trust, not for long
will that record be held,

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.093
but we're glad to have been the first.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:45.530
The project had really
broad participation,

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:49.420
across the Beinecke Library,
working with our colleagues

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:51.770
and the university printer's office.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.410
And I shout them out,

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:56.940
design of a fantastic
jotter and all that we do.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.390
John Gamble, Rebecca
Martz, their colleagues

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:02.720
help us at the Beineke
Library, to keep it real.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:07.040
And keeping it real was
a big aim of the effort.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.730
Beineke's director E.C. Schroeder,

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.660
charged us from the beginning

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.510
and encouraged us
throughout to stay to true

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:17.260
to our library's 2027
visions and aspirations,

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.307
which include and I quote,

00:06:19.307 --> 00:06:24.307
"The call to 'provide
all who enter virtually,

00:06:24.557 --> 00:06:28.787
"a level of service,
accessibility, and inspiration,

00:06:28.787 --> 00:06:32.827
"on par with that enjoyed on site.

00:06:32.827 --> 00:06:37.157
"That our digital be inspired by an on par

00:06:37.157 --> 00:06:38.570
"with our actual.'"

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:43.470
We had, as you may know,
in 2015, opened a new site

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.050
at 344 Winchester, state of the art home

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.520
for Beineke's technical services group.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.230
And then in 2016, the
iconic Bunshaft building

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.440
was reopened after complete renovation.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.470
And so in some ways, our work culminating

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.980
on February 21st of this year,

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:04.070
was following on with
the digital renovation,

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:07.293
just as we had had physical
renovations before.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:13.540
The work, the site,
aims to evoke the actual

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:18.320
and to provide clear paths
for the varied constituencies,

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.490
and they really are quite varied.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:23.240
We have 180,000 public visitors on site,

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:26.140
and obviously hundreds
of thousands online.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.600
We have hi-tech folk.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.200
We have low-tech folk.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:31.910
We have people who are doing research

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:34.680
across all the fields of human endeavor.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.690
We have faculty, we have students.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.420
We have people who see us here

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:40.440
and people who only engage us online.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.966
So it's a very varied audience.

00:07:42.966 --> 00:07:47.170
To keep it real, the
new look of the website,

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:50.160
matches, in the digital realm,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.251
the library's design systems for print,

00:07:53.251 --> 00:07:57.000
itself, informed by the
physical architecture

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.550
of the library that opened in 1963.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.590
On the site, in the footer,

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:04.950
we use Matthew Carter's
marvelous typeface,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.230
as it used throughout the university,

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:11.140
an anchor that ties us to
Yale University overall,

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:13.140
and to the Yale University library system

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:14.890
of which we are a part.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:17.350
And then, we use, as
you've hopefully seen.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.450
How many people have seen the new website?

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:22.960
Those who didn't raise
their hands are invited to,

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:24.360
but only after we're done.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.193
(audience laughs)

00:08:25.193 --> 00:08:26.740
And you've gone off airplane mode

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:28.040
and reopened your laptops.

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:34.030
We use a universe-condensed
family for titles and headers,

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:38.540
and that's a typeface that Rebecca uses

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:41.300
throughout our print to typeface the dates

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:44.090
to the era, mid-century modern era,

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:45.630
of the Beinecke's construction,

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.830
so it also again, evokes who we are

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:52.090
and matches with our physical
manifestations in print

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:53.143
and in swag.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:58.630
And then we use a bright, a cool warm red,

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:02.020
that is inspired by warm printer's red,

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.210
and again, a color, highlight color,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.270
that we use across our print

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:08.150
and that you'll see around the building

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:11.193
and in other things that
represent us as we go on the road.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:17.030
The results of keeping
it real are, we hope,

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.637
as it says in the library itself, quote,

00:09:19.637 --> 00:09:22.900
"an inspiration to all who enter."

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:26.780
It's a site that seeks, like the library,

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:30.830
to be both grounded and boundless.

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:34.157
Or, to invoke Stevens,

00:09:34.157 --> 00:09:38.077
"We keep coming back and
coming back to the real,

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:41.527
"To the hotel, instead of
the hymns that fall upon it

00:09:41.527 --> 00:09:43.467
"out of the wind.

00:09:43.467 --> 00:09:46.517
"We seek the poem of pure reality,

00:09:46.517 --> 00:09:50.467
"untouched by trope or
deviation, straight to the word.

00:09:50.467 --> 00:09:52.507
"Straight to the transfixing object,

00:09:52.507 --> 00:09:55.317
"to the object at the exactest point

00:09:55.317 --> 00:09:59.307
"at which it is itself,
transfixing by being purely

00:09:59.307 --> 00:10:01.007
"what it is.

00:10:01.007 --> 00:10:04.997
"A view of New Haven say,
through the certain eye.

00:10:04.997 --> 00:10:07.517
"The eye made clear of uncertainty,

00:10:07.517 --> 00:10:12.437
"with the sight of simple
seeing without reflection.

00:10:12.437 --> 00:10:16.387
"We seek nothing beyond reality.

00:10:16.387 --> 00:10:19.707
"Within it, everything,
the spirit's alchemicana,

00:10:19.707 --> 00:10:22.937
"included the spirit that goes roundabout,

00:10:22.937 --> 00:10:26.477
"and through included,
not merely the visible,

00:10:26.477 --> 00:10:30.313
"the solid, but the moveable, the moment.

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:35.163
We seek nothing beyond reality.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.653
Within it, everything.

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.030
On behalf of the Beinecke library,

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:45.180
I wanna publicly acknowledge and name

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:48.540
the people of Yale ITS,
whose collective labor

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.340
made the new website real.

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:53.660
Lisa Sawin, Dana Lipnickas, Franz Hartl,

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:56.410
Jevan Johnson, Taber
Lightfoot, Chao Hang Lu,

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.350
Vincent Massaro, Sylvia
Perez, Julie Ramantia,

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:02.470
Mark Saba and Heather White,
and also Rebecca Martz

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:04.810
in the university printer's office.

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:06.857
And I wanna acknowledge the people

00:11:06.857 --> 00:11:09.760
of the Beinecke library, who kept it real

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.270
and made this real.

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:13.490
In the interest of time,
I'm not gonna name them,

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:16.500
because it is everyone
of the Beineke Library,

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.190
who contributed ideas and time and talent

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:20.676
to this site.

00:11:20.676 --> 00:11:22.140
There're a 110 or so.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.030
I encourage you to read
their names on the website

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:25.903
after we're done.

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:32.480
By working with the Yale sites team,

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:35.723
we consciously chose to
bring this work home.

00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:38.590
It was a wise choice.

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:41.950
Outside vendors and
consultants can be great,

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:45.850
but there is a quantitative
and qualitative benefit

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:47.410
in working with people

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:50.323
who are both colleagues and neighbors.

00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:53.970
As a long time citizen of Yale,

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:57.960
I have been delighted to see
how the home-grown capacity

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:01.210
to create websites here has flourished.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:05.140
It's great now as a consumer and patron,

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:06.640
that the people that I rely on

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:10.570
for digital infrastructure
and design are nearby,

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:12.700
and that frankly they're people I run into

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:14.930
at the farmer's market in Wooster Square

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:17.280
and other places around town.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:20.770
Happily gone are the days
of back and forth email,

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:23.820
to set up a conference call a week out,

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:27.153
to schedule an onsite meeting a month out.

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:31.090
An instead, we have excellent assistance,

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:34.763
right next door, from real
people we really know.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.180
And bringing the work back
home also made a difference

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.060
in the results you see,

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:45.770
because all of the people
I named know the reality

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:48.770
and were informed, really informed,

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:51.100
by the reality of the library.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.180
All of our meetings happened on site,

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:57.150
in the library, and there's
something in the air

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.190
of the place.

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.180
The people who made this
site walked the walk

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:06.760
and breathed the air time
after time after time.

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.780
And I have to say, to shout out Dana,

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.330
and along with my colleagues,

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:14.201
I was delighted, not
only by the time we spent

00:13:14.201 --> 00:13:17.400
in a room together, but that she often,

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.220
with her laptop after meetings,

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:22.260
had concluded, stuck
around the courtyard level

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:25.960
and did her own work
there, remotely as it were,

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:28.490
but actually not remotely.

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:29.840
And I don't think that there's

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:34.640
a precise quantitative
measure that we could apply

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:37.860
to what that ministry of presence meant,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:42.600
but I am confident the
results speak for themselves

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:47.530
of being there, actually,
truly being there.

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:49.631
For you see, there is a virtue,

00:13:49.631 --> 00:13:54.351
with engaging reality,
that surpasses the delights

00:13:54.351 --> 00:13:57.080
of virtual reality.

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.270
Now the work we did together resulted

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:02.810
in a wonderful website
that does work well.

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:06.197
Some here, and part of me, are quants,

00:14:06.197 --> 00:14:07.820
and the quants will be happy to know

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:12.123
that we have solid data so
far to prove that it works.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:14.460
In the first 100 days,

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:17.829
which would be February 21 to May 31st,

00:14:17.829 --> 00:14:20.730
the number of unique views of the homepage

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:22.120
grew 5%.

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:22.953
And that's good.

00:14:22.953 --> 00:14:24.980
We obsess a bit about the homepage

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.390
because in our design thinking,

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.570
we really prioritized and privileged

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:32.510
those who don't know us.

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:36.640
And so we really sweated
that in particular.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:38.210
So increased traffic is good.

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:39.590
You don't want it going
in the other direction,

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:40.650
that's for sure.

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:42.740
But here's the better news.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:47.740
Average time on the
homepage is down by 33%,

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:51.700
while the bounce rate is down by 32%.

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:56.140
Long story short, not only
are more people coming,

00:14:56.140 --> 00:15:00.540
more people are more
quickly finding ways to go

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:03.640
and going there, rather
than if we again used

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:06.060
the real building, just going
through the revolving door

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.110
for a while and popping back out,

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:11.220
with never making it into the library.

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:14.930
So these quantitative measures
area really great news.

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:18.680
And the trend in the first 100
plus days has been very good.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:21.460
We also have qualitative testimony,

00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:23.360
in person and online.

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.900
My favorite comes from our
colleague Brita Belli OPAC,

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:30.310
who had the powerful
simple reaction on Twitter,

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:31.143
the fire emoji.

00:15:31.143 --> 00:15:33.410
(audience laughs)

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:38.343
It's good, if your design
merits the fire emoji.

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:44.050
And I'm even thinking that I may

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:49.050
use that as the sum total in
the year-end results column

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:52.809
of my managing at Yale
performance process review.

00:15:52.809 --> 00:15:54.130
(audience laughs)

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:55.230
So the reality is this,

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.470
more people are finding
their way more quickly

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:02.350
and more often to what really matters,

00:16:02.350 --> 00:16:04.950
the collection, the
programs, the exhibitions,

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:08.790
the people, the research, the scholarship,

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:10.620
of the library.

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:14.500
And in communications,
that's what really matters,

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:16.549
that's what really counts.

00:16:16.549 --> 00:16:20.960
We need to measure success,
not only in the metrics

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:22.910
that Google gives us,

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:25.930
and that social media analytics provide,

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:30.109
but the measure, sometimes
quite intangible, but real,

00:16:30.109 --> 00:16:33.970
is how well we serve to
welcome students and faculty

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:37.730
and researchers and public
visitors to the place,

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:42.360
so that they can engage,
confront, and build

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:44.700
on the rich record of human experience

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:47.470
and cultural achievement that we steward.

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:50.030
So a clearer, faster website means

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:53.980
we can better serve people
as they engage the past,

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:57.050
in the present, for the future.

00:16:57.050 --> 00:16:59.100
Perhaps our montra is

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:03.373
less time on website for
more time on real site.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:08.310
A clearer, faster website
is a means towards the end

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:11.430
of more people lingering,
wandering, exploring,

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:15.530
and looking for themselves
in the actual library.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:18.090
Some of you may now, I'm a Kentuckian.

00:17:18.090 --> 00:17:20.330
And Kentuckian's are always happy

00:17:20.330 --> 00:17:22.480
when other Kentuckian's are cited.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:24.190
So I was delighted to hear words

00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:27.047
of Hunter S. Thompson
yesterday, you will recall them.

00:17:27.047 --> 00:17:29.027
"Faster faster, until the thrill of speed

00:17:29.027 --> 00:17:30.767
"overcomes the fear of death!"

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:34.470
Now if you know brother
Hunter in his life,

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:36.790
he did know a little bit about speed,

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:39.525
both the motorcycle variety
and the amphetamine variety.

00:17:39.525 --> 00:17:40.830
(audience laughs)

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:44.850
And his life and living and it's ending

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:47.760
brings to mind another great saying,

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:51.934
not of Kentucky but of
the world, "Speed kills."

00:17:51.934 --> 00:17:55.667
So, this Kentuckian's
mantra might rather be,

00:17:55.667 --> 00:18:00.437
"Faster, faster, until you
get where you can slow down,

00:18:00.437 --> 00:18:04.440
"and think and engage with reality."

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:09.210
For you see, we seek
nothing beyond reality.

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:11.833
Within it, everything.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:15.540
And so for the Beinecke library,
a faster, better website

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:20.060
means more people might come
to see the real first printing

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:22.620
of the U.S. Declaration of Independence,

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:24.260
which will be on view again soon,

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:26.696
June 28th through July 11th.

00:18:26.696 --> 00:18:30.920
It is the first document of the nation,

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:35.920
printed on July 4, 1776,
one of 26 copies surviving,

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.490
of about 200.

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:43.400
Printed in Philadelphia
on that day, July 4, 1776,

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:47.780
and immediately sent out on
horseback and stagecoach,

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:50.260
throughout the states of the new nation.

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:55.260
It is the original viral
media of the nation.

00:18:55.350 --> 00:19:00.350
And to see that document is to
find nothing beyond reality,

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:03.433
and within it, everything.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:05.740
When it was on view last year,

00:19:05.740 --> 00:19:08.270
I happened upon a family who was visiting

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:10.970
from Toledo, Ohio, who had come to see

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:14.030
the Declaration of Independence on view.

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:16.590
The parents were standing
on either side of the case

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:19.300
reading it aloud, the full
declaration to each other

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:20.573
and to their two kids.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:24.417
One declared, as she read,

00:19:24.417 --> 00:19:27.187
"These words don't seem so old.

00:19:27.187 --> 00:19:31.510
"They seem very relevant right now."

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:34.383
Indeed, they are at once,

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:37.493
timeless and timely.

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:42.510
And that is a reality
that is most accessible

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:45.053
in the present of the real thing.

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:49.100
The papers in the Beinecke Library

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:51.340
and special collections
elsewhere on campus

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:54.530
and around the world are not dead trees.

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:56.303
They are living testimony.

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:00.715
They are actual evidence of who we were,

00:20:00.715 --> 00:20:03.560
who we are, and who we might be.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:06.770
And so a faster, better
website means more people

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:10.860
might come to see the first printing

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:15.860
of Frederick Douglass'
oration, given on July 5, 1852,

00:20:16.120 --> 00:20:19.690
to the Rochester Ladies'
Anti-slavery Society.

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:22.047
That is where Mr. Douglass asks,

00:20:22.047 --> 00:20:25.657
"What to the American
slaves your Fourth of July?"

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:28.700
And Mr. Douglass provided the answer

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:30.773
from the harsh reality he knew.

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:35.697
Mr. Douglass wrote, said, and is printed,

00:20:35.697 --> 00:20:38.287
"I answer a day that reveals to him more

00:20:38.287 --> 00:20:39.687
"than all the other days of the year,

00:20:39.687 --> 00:20:42.127
"the gross injustice
and cruelty to which he

00:20:42.127 --> 00:20:43.777
"is the constant victim.

00:20:43.777 --> 00:20:46.527
"To him, your celebration is a sham;

00:20:46.527 --> 00:20:48.417
"Your boasted liberty, an unholy license;

00:20:48.417 --> 00:20:50.947
"Your national greatness, swelling vanity;

00:20:50.947 --> 00:20:53.157
"Your sounds of rejoicing
are empty and heartless;

00:20:53.157 --> 00:20:56.597
"Your denunciations of tyrants,
brass-fronted impudence;

00:20:56.597 --> 00:20:59.977
"Your shouts of liberty and
equality hollow mockery:

00:20:59.977 --> 00:21:01.217
"Your prayers and hymns;

00:21:01.217 --> 00:21:03.167
"Your sermons and thanksgiving;

00:21:03.167 --> 00:21:05.407
"with all your religious
parade and solemnity

00:21:05.407 --> 00:21:09.667
"are, to Him, mere bombast,
fraud and deception, impiety,

00:21:09.667 --> 00:21:13.787
"and hypocrisy, a thin
veil to cover up crimes

00:21:13.787 --> 00:21:17.567
"which would disgrace
a nation of savages."

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:21.260
Words that echo today.

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:25.780
This is the 400th anniversary,

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:29.620
of when a ship set sail
with enslaved Africans,

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:31.063
headed to Jamestown.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.343
These are words that
are timely and timeless.

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:37.870
For you see friends,

00:21:37.870 --> 00:21:40.720
there is a virtue in confronting reality.

00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:45.500
Perhaps even more so,
when that reality itself,

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:48.120
is far from virtuous.

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:51.340
The reality accessible in repositories

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:53.240
like the Beinecke Library,

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:55.650
and peers across campus,
around that nation

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:57.290
and across the world,

00:21:57.290 --> 00:22:01.980
have the virtue of
encouraging us to remember.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:05.320
And they remind us that our nation's deeds

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:08.520
do not always match its words.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:10.398
And so a faster, better website,

00:22:10.398 --> 00:22:13.300
means that more people might encounter

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:16.930
the first printing of the
Women's Rights Convention

00:22:16.930 --> 00:22:21.930
at Seneca Falls, July 19th, 20th, 1848.

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:24.570
A report printed at the North Star Press,

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:26.880
Frederick Douglass' press,

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:30.480
and a report that includes
a declaration of sentiments,

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:33.050
which I think needs to
be much better known

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:35.360
as a document of the nation.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:38.887
171 years ago, the convention declared,

00:22:38.887 --> 00:22:41.187
"We hold these truths to be self-evident

00:22:41.187 --> 00:22:46.187
"that all men and women are
created equal," in 1848.

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:52.450
For words of the world
are the life of the world.

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:54.980
And a faster, better website

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:58.140
means more people might engage more deeply

00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:01.700
with the living legacy of Langston Hughes,

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:05.730
whose literary archives live in 672 boxes

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:07.473
at the heart of our campus.

00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:09.930
They might remember his words,

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:12.010
words that inspired Lorraine Hansberry

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:13.610
and Martin Luther King Jr.,

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:15.750
words that echo today.

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:18.690
What happens to a dream deferred?

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:21.040
And a faster, better
website means more people

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:23.470
might be inspired by how Hughes reckoned

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:25.650
with his reality,

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:28.660
how he confronted the
past and examined it,

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:33.047
and still concluded with an
eye to the future as he said,

00:23:33.047 --> 00:23:35.803
"Oh yes, I say it plain.

00:23:36.647 --> 00:23:39.787
"American never was America to me,

00:23:39.787 --> 00:23:42.427
"and yet I swear this oath.

00:23:42.427 --> 00:23:44.717
"America will be."

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:49.240
And so a faster, better
website makes it possible

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:52.860
to enable countless, real-time encounters

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:54.260
in the exhibition hall,

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:57.580
in the reading room, in the classrooms,

00:23:57.580 --> 00:24:00.943
as well as proxy-encounters
on the digital library.

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:06.470
That oft impalpable
impact is in many ways,

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:08.343
what counts the most.

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:13.370
As the brilliant Elizabeth
Alexander wrote in 2016,

00:24:13.370 --> 00:24:15.700
when the National Museum
of African American History

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:18.237
and Culture opened, and I quote,

00:24:18.237 --> 00:24:21.458
"Historians and curators are heroes.

00:24:21.458 --> 00:24:25.007
"The digging, the trench
work and smoothing,

00:24:25.007 --> 00:24:28.127
"the conjuration it
takes to piece together

00:24:28.127 --> 00:24:31.057
"filament by filament, a history,

00:24:31.057 --> 00:24:33.667
"and then to present it
so that we understand

00:24:33.667 --> 00:24:36.847
"and experience it as a living process,

00:24:36.847 --> 00:24:39.727
"is nothing short of wizardry."

00:24:39.727 --> 00:24:42.217
"History," Elizabeth wrote,

00:24:42.217 --> 00:24:45.567
"is not a triumphal
march to the finish line.

00:24:45.567 --> 00:24:49.447
"History is not all upturn
faces, nor ascendant.

00:24:49.447 --> 00:24:54.047
"History is made up of struggle,
contradiction, resistance."

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:58.663
Professor Alexander keeps
it real, and so must we.

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:03.660
Keeping it real also
means not being distracted

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:05.880
by the brightest, shiniest objects,

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:09.180
and not diluted by the disruption machine.

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:12.720
Yale alumna Jill Lepore got right

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:15.120
what the gospel of innovation gets wrong

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:18.737
in her 2014 New Yorker piece, and I quote,

00:25:18.737 --> 00:25:22.357
"Disruptive innovation
doesn't explain change.

00:25:22.357 --> 00:25:24.127
"It's not a law of nature.

00:25:24.127 --> 00:25:28.257
"It's an artifact of history,
an idea forged in time.

00:25:28.257 --> 00:25:30.967
"It's the manufacturer of a moment

00:25:30.967 --> 00:25:34.257
"of upsetting and edgy uncertainty.

00:25:34.257 --> 00:25:38.247
"Transfixed by change,
it's blind to continuity.

00:25:38.247 --> 00:25:41.630
"It makes a very poor profit."

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:45.093
Professor Lepore keeps
it real, so must we.

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:47.810
Spoiler alert!

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:51.410
The machines will not
save us by themselves.

00:25:51.410 --> 00:25:55.060
And the evidence is all around us to see.

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:57.640
Valerie Strauss headlined
in the Washington Post,

00:25:57.640 --> 00:25:58.797
couple of weeks ago,

00:25:58.797 --> 00:26:02.030
"New report on virtual
education: As it turns out,

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:03.810
it is to good to be true."

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:07.007
As Brett Stevens opined
in the Times on May 3,

00:26:07.007 --> 00:26:09.047
"Over the past several
years, we've learned a lot

00:26:09.047 --> 00:26:11.997
"about the unintended
consequences of social media.

00:26:11.997 --> 00:26:14.247
"Platforms intended to
bring us closer together,

00:26:14.247 --> 00:26:16.487
"make us angry and more isolated.

00:26:16.487 --> 00:26:18.247
"Platforms aimed and democratizing

00:26:18.247 --> 00:26:20.037
"speech-empowered demagogues,

00:26:20.037 --> 00:26:23.287
"Platforms celebrating
community violate our privacy

00:26:23.287 --> 00:26:26.927
"in ways we scarcely realize
and serve as conduits

00:26:26.927 --> 00:26:29.828
"for deceptions, hiding in plain sight."

00:26:29.828 --> 00:26:33.897
Max Boot, a couple of weeks
ago in the Post asked,

00:26:33.897 --> 00:26:37.270
"Are we becoming too stupid
to govern ourselves?"

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:39.767
And observed, "it's true that the internet

00:26:39.767 --> 00:26:41.527
"has put a lot of information online,

00:26:41.527 --> 00:26:43.667
"but like diners passing
up a healthy salad

00:26:43.667 --> 00:26:46.027
"for an artery-clogging cheeseburger,

00:26:46.027 --> 00:26:48.227
"many information consumers are instead,

00:26:48.227 --> 00:26:50.317
"digesting junk news."

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:54.442
And just the other day,
M.R. O'Connor reported

00:26:54.442 --> 00:26:56.220
on a new study.

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:59.540
She was writing, M.R. was
writing in The Washington Post

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:00.373
on June 5.

00:27:00.373 --> 00:27:05.220
The headline was, "Ditch the
GPS, it's ruining your brain."

00:27:07.937 --> 00:27:10.387
"When people use tools," the story says,

00:27:10.387 --> 00:27:14.067
"such as GPS, they tend to
engage less with navigation.

00:27:14.067 --> 00:27:17.207
"Therefore, brain area
responsible for navigation

00:27:17.207 --> 00:27:18.547
"is less used.

00:27:18.547 --> 00:27:22.080
"And consequently, their brain
areas involved in navigation

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:24.930
"tend to shrink."

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:25.833
Little bit scary.

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:30.823
So, at at time when machines
are gaining autonomy,

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:36.830
are we doing enough to protect
and promote human liberty?

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:41.770
In a time of deep fakes
and information overload,

00:27:41.770 --> 00:27:44.773
are we doing enough to keep it real?

00:27:46.050 --> 00:27:49.490
We surely can use quantum science,

00:27:49.490 --> 00:27:53.960
but we also must have quality humanities,

00:27:53.960 --> 00:27:57.763
if we are to have quality
of life as humans.

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:01.299
Libraries, museums, and universities stand

00:28:01.299 --> 00:28:05.010
as essential arsenals for
liberty and democracy.

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:09.370
They house the living
materials necessary to confront

00:28:09.370 --> 00:28:13.569
what Gore Vidal called, "The
United States of Amnesia,

00:28:13.569 --> 00:28:18.569
"where we learn nothing
because we remember nothing."

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:22.310
Libraries, museums, and
universities invite us

00:28:22.310 --> 00:28:26.840
to be alive in the world, to
call forth active citizens,

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:29.343
rather than passive spectators.

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:34.680
Libraries, museums, and
universities should keep it real.

00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:39.680
In an ethos, in a era when the
ethos of creative destruction

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:42.390
runs amuck in the markets,

00:28:42.390 --> 00:28:46.237
we should be in words
of President Salovey,

00:28:46.237 --> 00:28:49.287
"a place of creative construction,

00:28:49.287 --> 00:28:52.847
"a place of both innovation and tradition.

00:28:52.847 --> 00:28:55.417
"A place where we draw form the past,

00:28:55.417 --> 00:28:58.627
"in the present, to build the future."

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:02.147
President Salovey said
Yale embraces quote,

00:29:02.147 --> 00:29:03.227
"creative construction,

00:29:03.227 --> 00:29:05.747
"not orthodox adherence to the past,

00:29:05.747 --> 00:29:08.647
"nor the pursuit of innovation,

00:29:08.647 --> 00:29:12.763
"merely for its own sake,
but a dynamic tradition.

00:29:13.687 --> 00:29:16.909
"We don't just seek to make new things.

00:29:16.909 --> 00:29:19.397
"We seek to make things better."

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:25.260
Now as we collectively aim
to make both better things

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:28.360
and to make things better,

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:31.340
let me offer some
tangible, practical ideas

00:29:31.340 --> 00:29:34.183
applicable, perhaps to workplace culture.

00:29:35.770 --> 00:29:38.860
First, and maybe second and last,

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:39.963
get out more often.

00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:44.203
Like Dana, practice a
ministry of presence.

00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:48.360
Let's not all be stuck in
our cubicles or offices

00:29:48.360 --> 00:29:51.600
or hotel space, wherever we are routinely,

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:53.410
where we do the work.

00:29:53.410 --> 00:29:55.610
Use Zoom Rooms for sure,

00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:59.120
but also make real room in your schedule,

00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:03.010
to wander, to explore, to look closely

00:30:03.010 --> 00:30:06.650
at this extraordinary place to acknowledge

00:30:06.650 --> 00:30:11.650
what it is, its opportunities,
its challenges, its history.

00:30:11.660 --> 00:30:14.730
For it is in that, that we
can construct the future.

00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:16.690
If you're in ITS, as many are here,

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:18.630
communications like me,

00:30:18.630 --> 00:30:22.920
service providers to
departments and schools,

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:26.300
be like the Yale site's team was with us

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:27.800
at the Beinecke.

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:29.290
Really get to know the place.

00:30:29.290 --> 00:30:30.763
Be in it, be present.

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:35.880
Spend real time on site,
as we all create things

00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:37.433
to live online.

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:40.690
Go to the Grove Street Cemetery.

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:42.290
Go to the art gallery.

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:44.160
Go to the Center for British Art.

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:45.960
Go to the New Haven Museum.

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:47.423
Give yourself the time.

00:30:48.650 --> 00:30:51.890
It will pay off in many ways.

00:30:51.890 --> 00:30:54.790
And remember words of the world
are the life of the world.

00:30:58.076 --> 00:30:59.700
In the beginning was the word,

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:01.070
a book that's on permanent view

00:31:01.070 --> 00:31:03.287
at the Beinecke Library says,

00:31:03.287 --> 00:31:05.447
"Writing matters.

00:31:05.447 --> 00:31:09.567
"Writing really matters in
this place and for our world."

00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:12.360
And, since it's summer season,

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:14.820
some potential summer reading,

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:18.300
I have never gone wrong with Maria Popova,

00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:19.570
and "Brain Pickings."

00:31:19.570 --> 00:31:21.543
If you don't know it, read it.

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:26.407
And read a book or two or three.

00:31:26.407 --> 00:31:29.720
We should not be the kind of people,

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:33.713
who accept Bill Barr's blended reality.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:38.893
Read the actual Mueller Report.

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:44.977
Read my dear friend Casey Gerald's,

00:31:44.977 --> 00:31:47.400
"There Will Be No Miracles Here,"

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:51.720
a powerful memoir by
a recent alum of Yale,

00:31:51.720 --> 00:31:53.823
who definitely keeps it real.

00:31:56.320 --> 00:32:01.000
And/or read John Menta's history
of the Quinnipiac people,

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:03.330
on who's land we stand.

00:32:03.330 --> 00:32:05.600
Acknowledge not only that history,

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:07.483
but get to know that history.

00:32:08.500 --> 00:32:11.560
When you visit the Grove Street Cemetery,

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:15.250
pay your respects at
the life, at the grave

00:32:15.250 --> 00:32:18.650
of William Grimes, who in 1825

00:32:18.650 --> 00:32:21.250
wrote the first, first-person narrative

00:32:21.250 --> 00:32:25.360
and published it himself of a person

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:28.910
who escaped enslavement
and lived in this town,

00:32:28.910 --> 00:32:31.170
and is buried in our cemetery.

00:32:31.170 --> 00:32:33.270
At the Beinecke Library,
we have the first printing.

00:32:33.270 --> 00:32:36.500
His ancestors have ensured
that it remains in print.

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:39.980
A powerful, powerful document.

00:32:39.980 --> 00:32:43.720
And in this 400th
anniversary of the arrival

00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:47.120
of the first enslaved people on the shores

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:49.270
of what is now our country,

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:52.717
read Ibram Kendi's extraordinary book,

00:32:52.717 --> 00:32:54.830
"Stamped From The Beginning,"

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:58.650
to understand the reality on the ground

00:32:58.650 --> 00:33:02.970
and the ideas that shaped this nation

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:04.773
and that we live with today.

00:33:06.258 --> 00:33:10.540
Ibram Kendi does extraordinary
historic and archival work

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:14.070
and again shows that the
documents and the history

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:15.310
are not dead.

00:33:15.310 --> 00:33:17.640
It is still amongst us now.

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:20.703
So, on this ordinary morning in New Haven,

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:24.820
I encourage you to stay for
the rest of the conference,

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:26.870
but get out more often (audience laughs).

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:30.043
On this ordinary morning in New Haven,

00:33:30.910 --> 00:33:35.910
I think we all know we live
in extraordinary times.

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:40.940
And we need to acknowledge
that we live in times

00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:45.130
of existential challenge,
to what this nation is,

00:33:45.130 --> 00:33:46.693
to what this world is.

00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:50.540
And as, on this ordinary
morning in New Haven,

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:54.520
we acknowledge that we live
in extraordinary times,

00:33:54.520 --> 00:33:57.763
I encourage you, equip yourselves well.

00:33:59.150 --> 00:34:03.640
The world requires the
reality-based community

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:05.623
now more than ever.

00:34:07.470 --> 00:34:10.010
I shall neither insult your intelligence

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:11.643
nor assault your conscience.

00:34:12.700 --> 00:34:15.423
You know what to do.

00:34:16.780 --> 00:34:21.543
Seek nothing beyond reality,
within it, everything.

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:25.506
Thank you.

00:34:25.506 --> 00:34:28.506
(audience applause)

00:34:40.522 --> 00:34:43.605
(light gentle music)

